<section class="section"><div class="container"><div class="eyebrow">Security and privacy</div><h1 class="section-title">Controls designed for a student healthcare-coordination prototype.</h1><p class="section-sub">The project uses common web security controls while clearly stating where production healthcare deployment would require stronger formal review.</p><div class="cards"><div class="card"><h3>Secure authentication</h3><p>Password hashing, MFA OTP, failed-login rate limiting and a 20-minute inactivity timeout.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Request protection</h3><p>CSRF tokens protect POST forms, prepared statements are used for database input and role checks protect restricted pages.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Minimum disclosure</h3><p>Donors remain anonymous in emergency matching until they accept. Organ-interest records require an additional hospital permission.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Auditability</h3><p>Authentication-sensitive events, administrative actions, donor responses and protected-detail access are recorded for review.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Local development mode</h3><p>Email/SMS actions can be simulated locally so the academic prototype can be demonstrated without exposing real service credentials.</p></div><div class="card"><h3>Clinical boundary</h3><p>The system does not perform compatibility, screening, transfusion approval, organ allocation or other medical decisions.</p></div></div></div></section>
